<h2>Why Homeowners Claims Get Complicated</h2>
<p>Hays County has a long record of severe spring weather. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports in the county since 1967, most of them in April and May. On May 9, 2024, the NWS logged <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181409" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.5-inch hail near the San Marcos outlet mall</a> and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy</a> west of the city. When a storm like that crosses a neighborhood, insurers often send adjusters who are handling many claims at once, and the first estimate can be built quickly.</p>
<p>Local construction adds its own wrinkles. Newer subdivisions along the I-35 corridor are mostly composition shingle roofs on slab foundations. Hill Country homes to the west often have standing-seam or exposed-fastener metal roofs, stone veneer, and detached barns or workshops. Older houses in the historic neighborhoods near downtown may use materials that are hard to match. Each of these can change how a claim should be scoped and priced.</p>

