<h2>The Coverages in a Typical Homeowners Policy</h2>
<p>Many residential claims involve more than one coverage, and each is paid against its own limit. Knowing which part of the policy applies helps keep items from being overlooked.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it usually pays for</th><th>Often missed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dwelling (Coverage A)</td><td>The house itself: roof, walls, built-in fixtures, attached garage</td><td>Drip edge, flashing, roof vents, interior ceiling repairs tied to a roof leak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other structures</td><td>Fences, detached garages, sheds, barns, workshops</td><td>Metal outbuildings and fences hit in the same storm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal property</td><td>Furniture, clothing, electronics, appliances that are not built in</td><td>Items damaged by water or soot that were thrown away without a record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss of use / additional living expense</td><td>Extra costs of living elsewhere while the home is repaired</td><td>Mileage, extra food costs, pet boarding, storage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinance or law</td><td>Increased costs to meet current codes when repairing covered damage, up to its limit</td><td>Code-required items that the base estimate leaves out</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many Texas policies also carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often written as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page before you estimate what the claim may pay. Our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains how depreciation affects the first check.</p>

<h2>Landlords and Student Rental Properties Near Texas State</h2>
<p>San Marcos has a large rental market, much of it serving Texas State University students. Residential landlords face a few issues that owner-occupants do not:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Different policy forms.</strong> A rental house is often insured under a dwelling fire or landlord policy rather than a standard homeowners form. Coverage for causes of loss, contents, and liability can be narrower.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of rental income.</strong> If units cannot be occupied during repairs, fair rental value or rental income coverage may apply, depending on the policy. Leases, rent rolls, and move-out dates help document it.</li>
<li><strong>Tenant belongings.</strong> Tenants' property is generally a matter for their own renters policies, not yours. Keep your claim focused on the building and any landlord-owned appliances or furnishings.</li>
<li><strong>Access and timing.</strong> Coordinating inspections around tenants, semesters, and move-in dates can slow a claim. A clear schedule and a single point of contact help.</li>
</ul>
<p>Owners of larger apartment communities or HOA-managed buildings should see our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page, since those properties are usually insured under commercial policies.</p>

