<h2>How a San Marcos Public Adjuster Handles a Residential Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> We read the declarations page and policy form to identify coverages, deductibles, limits, and deadlines that apply.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection and documentation.</strong> We document roof, exterior, interior, and contents damage with photos and measurements, and use Matterport 3D imaging where it helps show a room or structure.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate.</strong> We prepare a line-item estimate in Xactimate, the same software most insurers use, so the two can be compared directly. See <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Presentation and follow-up.</strong> We submit the documentation, answer the insurer's questions, and track deadlines and requests.</li>
<li><strong>Additional damage.</strong> If more damage appears once repairs begin, we help document it as a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">claim supplement</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>A public adjuster does not repair your home. Texas rules under Insurance Code Chapter 4102 keep us out of the construction work on claims we adjust, so you choose your own contractor.</p>

<h2>Building Codes and Permits in San Marcos</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos has enforced the 2021 International Codes since April 1, 2023. Under a local amendment, a roof covering replacement does not need a permit unless the new roof is heavier than the existing covering. Other repairs may need permits through the city <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1000/Permit-Center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permit Center</a>. Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, and other nearby cities run their own permit offices, and Hays County does not issue residential building permits in unincorporated areas. Code requirements do not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades; that depends on your ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has more detail.</p>

<h2>What to Do in the First Days After Damage</h2>
<ul>
<li>Photograph and video every affected area before anything is moved or removed.</li>
<li>Prevent further damage: tarp openings, shut off water, and start drying. Keep every receipt.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and write down the claim number and adjuster's contact details.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials or samples when you can, especially flooring, cabinets, and unique items.</li>
<li>Track extra living costs if you cannot stay in the home.</li>
<li>Use our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> to stay organized.</li>
</ul>
<p>Public adjusters are not attorneys, and we do not give legal advice. For questions about lawsuits or legal deadlines, consult a Texas attorney. If you want a licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> to look at where your homeowners claim stands, start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
